  [1mThe fork:[0m how long the id survives, and who can read it. This is expensive to change later — it's the stored format,
  and any id already issued under the old scheme is either migrated or orphaned.

  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives tab close and browser restart, shared across tabs on the origin, never sent to the server
    automatically. Simple synchronous API, no expiry to manage. Costs: client-only, so the backend can't see the id
    unless you put it in a request body explicitly; and it lives forever until something clears it.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — same API, but scoped to one tab and erased when that tab closes. Cheap and privacy-light, but it
    does not really satisfy "it should persist," and two tabs would carry different ids.
  - [1mCookie[0m — persists with an explicit expiry you control, and rides along on requests to the origin so the backend sees
    it for free. Costs: it's sent on every request whether you want it or not, needs [38;5;153mSameSite[39m/[38;5;153mSecure[39m attributes handled
    correctly, and the size budget is tight.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m.[0m It matches "persists" and "works across the app" exactly, and since the backend is a
  stub, the automatic-transmission advantage of a cookie buys nothing today. When a real API arrives, passing the id in
  a request body is a small change; unwinding a cookie's transmission semantics later is not.

  One risk to flag before you pick, because it applies to any of these: a persistent, app-wide identifier whose stated
  purpose is tracking users is exactly what GDPR/ePrivacy-style consent rules cover, and cookie-vs-[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m doesn't
  change that — what matters is the purpose, not the mechanism. If this app has or will have users in those
  jurisdictions, consent and a deletion path are design constraints, not follow-ups. I'll put whatever you decide in the
  spec's constraints; I just don't want it discovered after the interface is set.

